>Hence "For newbies it's helpful.  Experts should, and will, be making
>their own decisions anyway."

But I still don't think that the game's use of colour should eventually
end up being misleading.

Here's another reservation I have about AC colouring: in most games
I've played, the use of red-yellow-green in conjunction with a meter
signifies a _deficiency_ in the statistic that the meter is monitoring.
Red typically signifies a dangerous deficiency in the statistic that
should be rectified if it is in the player's power to do so. Green
typically signifies the "optimal" range which the player ought to
return to.

This colour system works for the HP meter, as hit points  naturally
progresses from the red range to the green range as a matter of due
course. It doesn't work for AC because armour class improvement isn't
something that the player has direct control over. Either the game
gives you better armour, enchant armor scrolls, more money, a wish
etc., or you're stuck with a yellow AC readout.

>Right!  Newbies that start with all that unnecessary armor should
